	abstract = {Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for technical assistance, yet their reliability on highly specialized RF/microwave engineering queries is not well quantified. We introduce DCWF-QA, a focused benchmark for direct-coupled waveguide filters (DCWF) built from 214 peer-reviewed papers. We define six question types to cover the core information needs and typical failure modes in this domain: numeric specifications, structural/topology reasoning, transmission-zero analysis, tuning/manufacturing considerations, design-process workflows, and refusal/abstention for evidence-insufficient cases. Starting from 25680 LLM-synthesized candidates, expert screening yields 3107 curated questions with traceable paper identifiers. We evaluate representative LLMs under no-retrieval and article-level RAG settings using end-to-end answer accuracy (QA-Acc) and retrieval evidence recall (Hit@5). Results show that DCWF-QA is challenging for LLMs without retrieval, while RAG effectively improves answer correctness and achieves strong evidence coverage. Nevertheless, the overall performance of current models remains unsatisfactory with substantial room for improvement. This demonstrates that grounding is both measurable and beneficial for specialized engineering QA, and highlights the significance of DCWF-QA as a targeted evaluation framework to advance model capability in this niche domain.  © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
