	doi = {10.1007/978-981-92-5641-9_41},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055398923?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Standard Dempster–Shafer fusion computes the conflict mass K on a single fixed frame, returning one scalar per pair of sources. When the label space is a strict taxonomy such as the Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical (ATC) classification, this collapse hides whether the sources disagree about the broad anatomical group or only about the fine-grained chemical substance. We introduce hierarchical conflict mass (HCM), a multi-resolution diagnostic obtained by lifting K through the chain of coarsenings induced by the taxonomy. We identify a coarsening-coherence condition under which conflict mass is preserved under exact refinement/coarsening, and show that the practical projected construction used by HCM produces an approximately monotone multi-level profile empirically. From the profile we derive two summaries: a depth-of-agreement statistic D<sub>η</sub> and a level-weighted abstention score u<sub>HCM</sub>. We evaluated HCM in a MIMIC-III subset with three heterogeneous evidence sources and an operationally disjoint NDC-based reference pipeline, comparing against five baselines. HCM improves error-detection AUROC over flat conflict mass at every ATC level—with the highest gain around +0.07 at level 1 but a smaller +0.021 at level 3—and reduces level-3 selective risk at 80% coverage from 0.074 to 0.067, a modest but statistically significant change (p=0.034). The level-resolved profile additionally enables a level-graceful abstention rule that flat conflict cannot support. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.},
