	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044434158?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Named Entity Recognition (NER) for low-resource languages such as Telugu has seen rapid progress with transformer-based models; however, their predictive reliability remains underexplored. In safety-critical and real-world deployments, accurate confidence estimation and uncertainty-aware decision-making are as important as predictive accuracy.This paper presents a reliability-oriented evaluation framework for Telugu NER built upon a transformer-based token classification model using MuRIL. Rather than introducing a new architecture, the study focuses on three complementary aspects: (i) calibration analysis using Expected Calibration Error (ECE), Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL), and Brier Score; (ii) post-hoc probability calibration via temperature scaling; and (iii) selective prediction using entropy-based uncertainty filtering.We further extend token-level uncertainty handling by introducing span-consistent selective prediction aligned with BIO tagging constraints. Experimental results on the Telugu subset of the WikiAnn dataset demonstrate that temperature scaling improves probability calibration without degrading F1-score, while selective prediction enables improved precision under controlled coverage.The study highlights the importance of moving beyond accuracy-centric evaluation toward reliability-aware metrics in low-resource NER systems. The proposed evaluation protocol provides a practical foundation for deploying trustworthy Telugu NER models in real-world applications. © 2026 IEEE.},
