	abstract = {The rapid dissemination of misinformation on social media has spurred extensive research on automated fake news detection. Despite recent advancements in deep learning that have enhanced benchmark accuracy, a substantial gap persists between laboratory performance and real-world applicability. This survey offers a taxonomy-driven review of fake news detection methodologies and categorizes existing approaches into content-centric, context-centric, and reliabilitycentric paradigms. Content-based models utilize textual semantics through machine learning and transformer architectures but often depend on static representation. Context-centric approaches incorporate propagation dynamics using graph neural networks and temporal modeling but frequently rely on discrete time snapshots that hinder early detection under irregular diffusion patterns. Multimodal fusion methods integrate semantic and contextual signals; however, naive and confidence-unaware fusion strategies introduce additional vulnerabilities into the model. The survey further explores uncertainty quantification frameworks, including Bayesian methods and evidential deep learning, underscoring the importance of selective abstention and human-in-the-loop moderation for a trustworthy deployment. Two systemic limitations are identified: the discrete-time bottleneck in propagation modeling and the reliability deficit caused by overconfident inference. This study concludes by outlining the open challenges in early detection, cross-platform generalization, adversarial robustness, and uncertainty-aware decision support, emphasizing the need to transition from accuracy-driven classification to reliability-aware misinformation detection systems.  © 2026 IEEE.},
