	abstract = {The integration of AI data centers into the power grid represents one of the most emerging and complex challenges for energy systems. As computational demand scales at an unprecedented rate, the traditional grid planning study's paradigm of treating data centers as rigid, inflexible loads is becoming economically, mathematically and operationally untenable. This work aims to understand and address the large load interconnection bottleneck by modeling and evaluating AI load flexibility. By examining data center's temporal and spatial shifting capabilities within a grid capacity expansion framework, we build a quantitative grid planning model, and evaluate their impacts on additional generation, operational costs, and network congestion. A numerical study reveals interesting observations, as AI data center flexibility are not felt consistently, and increasing flexibility does not necessarily translate to less required generation capacity. Depending on data center's locations, flexibility range, and grid load conditions, flexible AI load can help reduce grid investment and operational costs by. Our work also indicates that longer deferral window of AI compute has diminishing returns in terms of offloading grid electricity dispatch pressure.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
