	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046214082?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Automated interpretation of dairy cow vocalizations can support precision livestock farming by providing non-invasive indicators of welfare-relevant behavioural context; however, real-barn audio is highly variable, noisy, and uncertainty-prone. This paper presents a confidence-aware multi-expert late-fusion framework for macro-behaviour classification of cow vocalizations into nine operational groups (G0-G8). The framework combines three complementary specialists: a Call Signature Expert that models clip-level acoustic signatures using global descriptors, a Window Pattern Expert that captures short-time motifs from 3 s windows using frozen PANNs CNN14 embeddings, and an Event Histogram Expert that represents each clip as a Bag-of-Audio-Words distribution over learned acoustic events. Expert posterior probabilities are concatenated and fused using an XGBoost meta-classifier to produce the final macro-group distribution and confidence score. On 569 real-barn clips using a 455/114 train/test split, late fusion achieved 0.816 accuracy and 0.639 Macro-F1, outperforming all individual experts. Additional analyses show that the 3 s non-overlapping configuration provided the strongest fusion Macro-F1 among tested window settings, while confidence-thresholded abstention improved reliability by enabling a controllable coverage-accuracy trade-off. Calibration analysis further showed that the fusion confidence was useful but imperfect (ECE = 0.100; multiclass Brier = 0.294), supporting its use as a practical deployment-oriented confidence signal rather than a full uncertainty model. These results indicate that integrating global, local, and distributional acoustic evidence with selective prediction is an effective and pragmatic strategy for macro-behaviour recognition in noisy farm environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
