	abstract = {ISO/IEC 25010 defines eight product quality characteristics, yet after decades of research, a persistent gap remains between quality definitions and practical measurement. We argue this gap exists not because the problem is intractable, but because previous approaches relied primarily on structural metrics, which struggle to capture semantic dimensions of quality. This paper articulates a vision for closing this gap through triangulation: combining evolutionary signals from repository mining, semantic interpretation from LLM agents, and structural analysis from validated metrics. Central to this vision is a formalized concept: when signals disagree, the disagreement becomes a typed diagnostic signal, revealing not just uncertainty, but which quality dimension requires attention and what action is appropriate. We present an initial formalization of disagreement as calibrated, confidence-aware divergence across signals, an ISO mapping mechanism that combines rules with constrained LLM judgments and abstention, and a triangulation architecture that integrates with verification and repair workflows. Triangulation serves as a Verification-Repair substrate: an evidence layer that gates verification tasks and guides repair actions. If this vision succeeds, quality assessment transforms from opaque numbers to actionable, uncertainty-aware guidance. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
	type = {Conference paper},
