	abstract = {Dynamic tariffs require forecasts of how consumption responds to price signals not yet observed. We develop a Markov-Switching Seasonal Autoregressive model with Exogenous variables (MS-SARX) that endogenously identifies latent consumption regimes and estimates regime-dependent price elasticities via an L<sub>2</sub>-regularised Expectation-Maximisation algorithm. Evaluated on operational smart-meter data from Denmark’s largest distribution system operator across three case studies, MS-SARX reduces root mean square error by 6–22 % at the primary substation level relative to the strongest baseline per case, with the largest gains where consumption exhibits multimodal regime structure. Embedding these forecasts in a revenue-neutral tariff optimisation yields model-implied peak-load reductions of 9–49 % and potential infrastructure deferral savings of 0.7–47.7 million DKK. These tariff and economic outcomes are simulation-based and contingent on the stability of regime-specific price response under tariffs not yet observed; field validation remains necessary. While demonstrated on a Danish DSO, the framework transfers to other tariff regimes, countries, and customer mixes wherever operational smart-meter and price data are available. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
