	abstract = {Machine learning models have demonstrated strong performance in breast cancer classification; however, most existing approaches rely on forced predictions for all samples, which can be risky in safety-critical clinical settings [6]. In practice, misclassification of ambiguous cases may lead to severe consequences, particularly in automated decision-support systems [7]. This paper presents a decision-aware machine learning framework that increases prediction reliability by integrating confidence-based abstention into the classification process. The proposed framework allows the model to defer decisions when predictive confidence falls within an uncertain range, thereby reducing the risk of erroneous predictions. Logistic Regression and Random Forest classifiers are evaluated on the Breast Cancer Wisconsin dataset [1] using a strict train-validation-test protocol. Experimental results show that while baseline models achieve high overall accuracy, the decision-aware framework eliminates misclassifications on high-confidence cases at the cost of reduced coverage. On the test set, the framework achieves perfect accuracy on decided samples while abstaining from a small fraction of ambiguous cases. These findings demonstrate that confidence-aware decision policies can improve the reliability of machine learning systems for clinical decision support without modifying the underlying classifier. © 2026 IEEE.},
