	doi = {10.1016/j.ijar.2026.109797},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047672300?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Scalar confidence scores cannot distinguish uncertainty just introduced from uncertainty accumulated silently across a multi-step pipeline. We propose a set-valued stochastic framework in which the pre-intervention state is a random compact convex set Y<sub>i</sub>⊂R<sup>d</sup> and safety is the exact inclusion Y<sub>i</sub> ⊆ S<sub>i</sub> . Prediction uses an affine set transformer and observation filtering uses a label-preserving coefficient-hull zonotope enclosure. The resulting mean-width shrinkage factor decomposes into propagated and newly introduced ambiguity components, exposing an epistemic bottleneck that scalar monitoring cannot localize. The state recursion is defined only while prediction and observation remain feasible; an empty intersection triggers halting, abstention, or a new observation rather than an artificial post-failure state. We prove measurability of the polyhedral filter, state the integrability conditions needed for Aumann conditional expectations, and derive conditional mean-width, maximal, block-contraction, Lyapunov-exponent, tube-exit, and resolution bounds. A reference-state coverage assumption yields a finite-horizon probability bound for feasibility failure. We also quantify coefficient-hull conservatism and give exact box-inclusion tests that retain directional information. A deterministic contract-processing case identifies a liability-risk failure hidden by scalar scores. A reproducible Monte Carlo experiment with 10<sup>5</sup> paths verifies conditional contraction (ρ=0.88), resolution-tail and maximal bounds, a negative empirical log-growth rate, and the feasibility-failure bound. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.},
