	abstract = {Task and Motion Planning (TAMP) can generate symbolically and geometrically feasible plans for heterogeneous dual-arm manipulation, but these plans are commonly executed as sequential action streams, leaving one manipulator idle for substantial portions of the task. In shared workcells, this is often conservative: many steps are weakly coupled in space and can overlap safely, while others require strict synchronization, such as handovers. To address this conservatism, this paper proposes a post-plan layer that parallelizes execution of a sequential symbolic–geometric plan without modifying the underlying solver, and evaluates it in simulation and on a heterogeneous dual-Franka Panda platform on cooperative pick–place, insertion, and handover assembly tasks. A Satisfiability solver (SAT) is used as a fast feasibility engine to compute a structure-preserving macro-interleaving of object-centric manipulation groups that increases cross-robot switching points under precedence, single-robot exclusivity, and handover constraints. Candidate overlaps are then proposed at the primitive level and checked through collision-aware geometric validation; unsafe overlaps are removed by local deferral. The pipeline is evaluated against a sequential baseline in simulation and on physical hardware. In simulation, scheduling overhead remains low while execution time is reduced by up to 19.8% in six-object scenes. On a hardware platform performing cooperative assembly and handovers, execution-time reductions of 20%–25% are obtained with modest additional computation. These results indicate that post-plan SAT interleaving combined with geometric validation provides a practical mechanism for exploiting parallel execution in heterogeneous dual-manipulator TAMP. Experiment videos are available at: https://youtu.be/Pzjnzdlk-1Q. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
