	abstract = {Cloud services with query timeouts produce right-censored latency data: the most extreme observations - precisely those that determine tail risk - are hidden behind the timeout boundary. We frame this as a problem of safe deployment screening under unavoidable censoring, not a general solution to small-sample tail prediction. We show that timeout-induced censoring causes every standard tail-latency estimator to miss 100% of true service-level objective (SLO) violations in screening, creating a dangerous blind spot for capacity planning. We present CenEVT, which treats timed-out queries as censored evidence rather than discarding them, fitting a penalized Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD) via censored maximum-likelihood estimation (MLE) to predict the 99th-percentile (p99) latency from small pilot samples (50-200 queries). A confidence-interval (CI) based decision rule, augmented with a regime-gated abstention for extreme censoring, flags SLO risk when uncertainty warrants caution. Evaluated on 618,660 experiment rows from two TSDB engines (TimescaleDB, ClickHouse) across six query templates, CenEVT detects 99.9% of true violations (0.06% false-safe rate) where all censoring-blind baselines miss 100%, with a quantile score (QS) 6.7× lower than naive methods. Against the strongest censored parametric baselines (log-normal, Weibull) the gap is operational: across the corpus CenEVT misses 27 fewer true SLO violations than censored log-normal, the gap ×concentrated in the subtle regime (low timeout rate, tight SLO). The censoring problem we identify is not specific to TSDBs - it affects any cloud service where requests are killed at a deadline. © 2026 IEEE.},
