	abstract = {Alert prioritisation (AP) is crucial for security operations centres (SOCs) to manage the overwhelming volume of alerts, ensure timely threat detection and minimise alert fatigue. Although predictive AI can process large alert volumes efficiently, it often struggles with evolving and uncertain cases that require human judgement. Human-AI teaming (HAT) offers a promising solution, which can be operationalised through learning to defer (L2D), where uncertain cases are deferred to human analysts. However, existing L2D models rely on static deferral policies that do not adapt to evolving cases based on human feedback. To address this limitation, we propose learning to defer with human feedback (L2DHF), that dynamically refines deferral decisions using human feedback in real-time. We evaluate L2DHF against baseline and state-of-the-art (SOTA) models on the UNSW-NB15 and CICIDS2017 datasets. Results show that L2DHF achieves superior AP accuracy and reduces analyst workload effectively, resulting in the highest overall performance among the evaluated models. This leads to improved threat detection and reduced analyst fatigue. © 2026 The Authors},
