	abstract = {Large Language Models (LLMs) frequently produce hallucinations, factually incorrect statements that undermine trust in generative AI. Existing adaptive verification methods suffer from three limitations: they rely on simulated retrieval rather than retrieval from a controlled reference corpus, lack rigorous comparison against controlled reference baseline approximations, and fail to provide actionable outputs when verification fails. This paper introduces VICTOR (Verification with Intelligent Confidence Thresholding and Optimal Retrieval), a framework that addresses these gaps through three contributions. First, VICTOR integrates BM25-based retrieval over a controlled reference corpus, providing a reproducible retrieval setup as an alternative to simulated lexical overlap. Second, it employs token-level entropy from LLM logprobs to dynamically adjust verification depth per claim. Third, unlike methods that only detect hallucinations, VICTOR combines BM25 retrieval, token entropy, revision, and abstention (refusing to answer when evidence is insufficient). Experimental evaluation on FEVER, HaluEval, and TruthfulQA demonstrates that VICTOR achieves high recall while maintaining competitive precision compared to other baseline methods. A full component ablation study across all three datasets shows that uncertainty-driven routing and BM25 retrieval provide clear benefits on FEVER and TruthfulQA, with dataset-dependent effects observed across components. An extended GPU-based evaluation against three recent hallucination mitigation methods (CoVe, SH2, and CEDA) confirms that VICTOR achieves the highest recall on each of the three evaluated datasets. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
