	abstract = {The rising global demand for chemotherapy, combined with the limited capacity of chemotherapy units, highlights the need for efficient treatment planning. We address the problem of assigning multiple treatments of each patient to specific days and chairs over a fixed planning horizon, while accounting for uncertainty in treatment durations. The model incorporates time window constraints and nurse workload limitations, and is formulated as a two-stage stochastic mixed-integer program. Deviations such as earliness, tardiness, within-window delays, deferrals to the next planning period, and overtime are penalized in the objective function. To address the computational burden of large scenario sets, we develop problem-based scenario reduction algorithms by extending a recent method from the stochastic programming literature. Using real data from an outpatient chemotherapy center, we evaluate our approach against alternative problem-based scenario reduction techniques and practical benchmark heuristics. To better reflect practice, we implement the approach within a rolling horizon framework. Finally, we perform sensitivity analyses and assess the value of stochastic solution. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
