	abstract = {Indonesian grammatical error correction (GEC) requires decisions about when to apply a proposed edit under synthetic-to-authentic domain shift. We evaluate a selective correction framework using a reconstructed, fully executable experiment. An audit of 1,955 authentic records removed 36 incomplete pairs, 14 duplicates, 45 string identities, and five additional token identities, leaving 1,855 pairs. Exact-text groups were partitioned into 1,297 training, 280 validation, and 278 test pairs. Synthetic priors were learned from 200,000 public development pairs. A cross-fitted logistic ranker combined synthetic mappings, authentic templates, spelling retrieval, and local language-model evidence. Thresholds were chosen on validation data under joint degradation and overcorrection budgets. At the 2% budget, the gate accepted five source edits, four correct: precision 80.00%, F0.5 4.03, coverage 1.80%, and word-error reduction 0.88%. No test source was degraded, but one of 278 correct-target controls changed. The precision interval was 37.55–96.38%. Removing the synthetic channel reduced available gold candidates from 110 to 109 and accepted true positives from four to three. Replacing the language-model feature with frozen IndoBERT produced 66.67% precision at 2.16% coverage, with one degraded source and one changed control. The results support restrictive authorization on this corpus, show limited unique synthetic contribution, and expose a substantial candidate-recall bottleneck. They do not establish broad correction ability or readiness for educational deployment. © (2026), (Science and Information Organization). All Rights Reserved.},
