	abstract = {Linguistic diversity, colloquial expression, and complexity in legal language limit access to legal information for the common man in India. Current large language models often produce hallucinated answers, raising concerns around trust, transparency, and safe deployment of generative AI in law. This paper presents a Tamil-first, bilingual voice-enabled system for equitable access to Indian law. The system handles Tamil and code-mixed queries using a Tamil Legal Lexicon and employs Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with strict constraints, ensuring responses are generated only from retrieved legal sources. When sufficient legal evidence is unavailable, the system abstains. The architecture follows a privacy-by-design approach, where user queries are processed without storage. Experimental results across 53 real-world citizen legal queries show a near four-fold improvement in domain-level retrieval performance (Recall@k from 0.20 to 0.80) and consistent abstention behavior when authoritative legal evidence is insufficient, in contrast to general-purpose systems such as GPT-4o and Copilot, which favor response completeness over safety and rarely abstain. The results highlight the importance of abstention as a safety mechanism in legal AI systems. The proposed approach provides a scalable framework for multilingual, citizen-centric legal assistance. © 2026 IEEE.},
