	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044410675?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Adversarial perturbations can induce clinically implausible model behavior while remaining visually subtle, motivating attack-agnostic screening of unreliable predictions at inference time. This paper proposes a density-consistency framework that characterizes the decision mechanism of a trained model in an explanation-space derived from feature-attribution maps such as SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP). The organizing principle is that clean inputs form locally consistent structures in this space, whereas adversarially perturbed inputs induce mechanism drift that departs from them. Operationally, the framework screens inputs with a lightweight detector that leaves the base classifier unchanged and is trained on clean data and a single attack family; a training-free density-consistency score provides a weaker complementary signal and defines the surrogate that an adaptive adversary targets. Screening supports two modes: rejection for conservative safety, and prediction sanitization to reduce false alarms without excessive abstention. A density-consistent adaptive threat model is formalized, an explicit adaptive-attack objective is provided, and defense-time and adaptive-attack complexity are analyzed. On chest radiographs across five clinical findings, a detector trained on one attack generalizes, without retraining, to seven unseen attack families, achieving an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of at least 0.987. Unlike feature-space statistics such as Mahalanobis distance, local intrinsic dimensionality, energy, and kernel density, the explanation-space detector retains a high worst-case AUROC on minimal-perturbation attacks. All results use a matched encoding protocol that rules out a compression confound, under which the detector attains a stable multi-seed AUROC of at least 0.997. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
