	abstract = {Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is rapidly being integrated into software engineering practice, potentially enhancing productivity. However, concerns have been raised about the long-term implications of GenAI integration. For students preparing to enter the profession, GenAI use may shape how they see themselves as future software engineers. While research has focused on instrumental benefits and drawbacks of GenAI, relatively little is known about how GenAI affects computing identity, including how students perceive competence, recognition, and responsibility in relation to others. This paper reports on an interview study with nine final-year master’s students that examines how they negotiate their computing identity when using GenAI. Our findings indicate that GenAI can strengthen computing identity by offering non-judgmental support and helping students to sustain progress and confidence in their work. Yet GenAI use can also threaten computing identity by creating dependence and anxiety about human relevance and recognition in professional settings. Students report navigating these tensions through strategies such as collaborative problem-solving, intentional abstention, and audit and quality control to align GenAI use with professional values and maintain legitimacy. These findings may have implications for designing educational practices that support students as GenAI becomes increasingly central to software engineering education and practice. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
