	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047858183?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Budgeted small-to-large cascades reduce LLM serving cost by letting a small language model (SLM) answer locally and escalating only a limited fraction of queries to a larger model. Existing approaches largely optimize the online routing rule, implicitly assuming that the SLM already provides a meaningful retain-versus-escalate score. We show that this assumption fails in a structured and deployment-critical way. In a two-coordinate failure geometry, the most damaging cases lie in an overtrust region: the base SLM assigns high confidence, while teacher-supported evidence indicates that the trajectory is unsafe to keep. These overconfident bypasses leave wrong local answers on the retained path despite an available fallback budget. We propose TAD-CL (Teacher-Anchored Disagreement-weighted Confidence Learning), a confidence-learning framework that reshapes this score geometry before routing. A bounded query-adaptive construction rule spends fewer teacher-scored trajectories on internally consistent queries and retains a larger supervision budget for ambiguous ones. The teacher anchor then defines a soft local-retention target from answer verification and LLM support, while a one-sided disagreement weight amplifies only the positive overtrust gap [ωbase−c<sup>*</sup>]+[jls-end-space/], where the frozen SLM is more confident than the teacher target permits. This targeted surrogate emphasizes teacher-identified overtrust while retaining a base fitting loss on every trajectory. The learned trajectory-conditioned confidence is then served by a lightweight SLM-first cascade with a validation-calibrated budget gate. Across MATH, GSM8K, and ARC-Challenge with three SLM families, TAD-CL improves over MaxProb and RouteLLM under matched LLM call-rate budgets. In the core two-backbone setting, it improves RouteLLM by 1.4–4.2 accuracy points. Mechanistic analysis reduces calibration error from 0.230 to 0.061 and unconditional bypass rate from 34.0% to 12.3% on the MATH pilot. An ASQA extension further demonstrates graded semantic verification beyond exact-match reasoning. These results suggest that reliable collaboration depends not only on the serving-time router, but also on learning a confidence geometry in which unsafe high-confidence local answers are less likely to bypass the LLM. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
