	abstract = {Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in task-based dialogue, such as planning, scheduling, and analytical decision support, where users often infer successful collaboration from fluent and cooperative responses. In this paper, we present a focused empirical study showing that such fluency can mask failures to maintain shared task interpretation across turns. We examine two task-based dialogue settings-constraint-based scheduling and definition-driven risk analysis-across multiple LLMs. While some models consistently satisfy constraints in scheduling dialogues, they do not make their interpretation of those constraints visible to the user. In contrast, definition-driven dialogues exhibit systematic breakdowns across all models, with failures manifesting as polite clarification deferral rather than hallucination or refusal. These findings highlight an interactional visibility problem and motivate the design of task-based dialogue systems that make assumptions, constraints, and interpretations explicit to support more reliable human-AI collaboration. © 2026 Association for Computing Machinery. All rights reserved.},
