	abstract = {Screening the large composition space of lead-free halides by first-principles calculations is costly. Composition-based models can help prioritize calculations, but their value depends on performance in unfamiliar chemistry. Using 1931 A<sub>2</sub>BB'X<sub>6</sub> records from the Materials Project spanning 353 B/B′ pairs, we combined grouped validation, selective prediction, external classification tests and analysis of existing DFT calculations. Complete-pair holdout increased the mean absolute error (MAE) of the Hurdle ExtraTrees reference model from 0.256 to 0.515 eV relative to random-row validation. On the same folds, Magpie–random-forest regression achieved 0.431 eV MAE. Retaining 80% of predictions by tree dispersion reduced the retained-set MAE to 0.305 eV. When targeting Materials Project-reported gaps of 1.1–1.8 eV, dispersion-assisted ranking increased target hits from 33 to 54 at 100 checks compared with gap-only ranking, with a paired 95% interval of 5–30 additional hits. This gain depended on the verification budget. In external tests of the reference classifier, accuracy remained higher for pairs represented in training after excluding Fe compounds. DFT analysis of three compounds showed direct gaps for Rb<sub>2</sub>LiInBr<sub>6</sub> under PBE and PBE + SOC, while gap interpretation for K<sub>2</sub>AgFeCl<sub>6</sub> and Rb<sub>2</sub>AgFeCl<sub>6</sub> depended on electron-correlation treatment and band occupations. These results support more efficient allocation of computational resources in lead-free halide screening and provide an auditable basis for model use under incomplete chemical coverage. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
