	abstract = {The rapid growth of residential electric vehicle (EV) adoption is reshaping distribution networks, shifting load patterns from relatively dispersed background demand to synchronized, high-amplitude charging events. When charging is unmanaged, these concentrated loads can exceed thermal and voltage limits, accelerate transformer aging, intensify harmonic stress, and prompt costly infrastructure upgrades. Unlike prior reviews that examine technical, economic, and coordination aspects separately, this paper brings them together within an integrated technical-economic analytical review that considers thermal limits, voltage security, and battery state-of-charge constraints simultaneously. Transformer loss-of-life, harmonic-thermal interactions, peak amplification, and renewable-EV timing mismatch are quantitatively analyzed alongside infrastructure deferral benefits and ancillary-service revenues. Economic performance is evaluated by balancing upgrade deferral, V2G revenue, battery degradation costs, and coordination overhead. A comparative assessment of ARIMA, LSTM, and reinforcement learning methods highlights practical trade-offs between forecasting accuracy and computational scalability under evolving charging behavior. Evidence from field pilots reports 10-30% peak reduction, 20-65% charging cost savings, and 100-500 per EV-year gross V2G revenue, depending on market structure and incentives. Overall, this review provides an integrated analytical foundation to support resilient and economically efficient EV-grid integration under high-penetration conditions. © 2026 The Authors.},
