	abstract = {Pneumonia is a serious respiratory infection that needs to be diagnosed in a timely and precise manner. Although the main diagnostic tool is the Chest X-Ray (CXR) imaging, the interpretation is still very difficult and subjective. Although deep learning models are performing well, most of the existing methods are not clinically reliable and interpretable. The paper introduces a confidence-calibrated, explainable decision-support system to detect pneumonia, CXR image. The system uses a well-tuned ResNet-18 classifier, uses temperature scaling to post-hoc confidence calibration, and uses a selective prediction strategy to refer low-confidence cases to expert review. Visual explanations created by Gradientweighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM), indicating diagnostically relevant parts of the image. This framework gives a test accuracy of 90.71, decreases the Expected Calibration Error (ECE) to 0.0548, and appropriately rejects 47 among 624 test samples due to uncertainty. These results indicate that classification combined with calibration and selective deferral and explainability within the same pipeline can greatly enhance clinical plausibility of AI-assisted diagnosis of pneumonia. © 2026 IEEE.},
