	abstract = {Accurate pea leaf disease classification is important for early crop monitoring, but practical agricultural diagnosis also requires reliable confidence estimation. A model that has a high confidence but low accuracy may lead to a wrong disease management decision, particularly when symptoms are similar for different disease classes. In this work, we present a confidence-calibrated selective prediction (CCSP) framework, called CalibPea-CaFormer, for trustworthy pea leaf disease classification. Pea leaf images were classified into four classes: Fresh Leaf, Downy Mildew Leaf, Leaf Miner Leaf, and Powder Mildew Leaf using a pretrained CaFormer-S18 backbone. Deterministic resizing and normalization of input images were performed without any custom design disease-specific pre-processing or attention modules. Data was partitioned by grouping duplicate and near-duplicate samples to minimize optimistic bias, and the held-out test set was not used in training or checkpoint selection. The model's performance on the held-out test partition was 96.67% accuracy, 96.50% macro-F1, 96.66% weighted-F1, 96.50% balanced accuracy, 0.955 Cohen's kappa, and 0.955 Matthews correlation coefficient. In addition to classification performance, predictive reliability was assessed with a reliability diagram and expected calibration error. The model had an ECE of 0.029, which is a good measure of the confidence-accuracy alignment. In addition, a selective prediction strategy was developed to assist with accept, review, and defer-to-expert decisions for uncertain samples. The results demonstrate that CalibPea-CaFormer offers a trust-aware diagnostic framework, integrating disease recognition with confidence-based decision support. © 2026 IEEE.},
