	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051413449?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Venous access is a critical preliminary step in numerous clinical procedures, yet in resource-limited settings it remains highly dependent on operator experience. Existing AI-assisted methods predominantly focus on enhancing vessel visualization, implicitly adopting the assumption that visible vessels are suitable for puncture. In practice, clinically critical contraindications, including scar tissue, telangiectasia, and cutaneous lesions, are seldom addressed at the decision level, and poor image quality creates a separate source of uncertainty. We propose Vein-SmartSite, a safety-oriented multimodal decision framework for venipuncture assistance that adopts a “perception–gating–abstention” scheme. The decision process is decoupled into three formally defined stages: structured vascular perception, constrained candidate point generation, and uncertainty-aware multimodal safety gating. A structured prompt design constrains the multimodal large language model (MLLM) to operate in a bounded output space, enabling reliable zero-shot deployment in safety-critical clinical decision-making. A conservative multi-source confidence design combines segmentation-, geometry-, and MLLM-level cues to govern abstention. The evaluation uses 523 image records. A 38-case expert-point-labeled fixed-test subset supports point-localization and framework-behavior analyses, while 519 cases form the nurse-consensus safety cohort for safety classification. Candidate-point generation attains a median Top-1 point error of 13.15 px and a Top-3 hit rate of 65.79% on the expert-point-labeled fixed-test subset. After abstention, the full safety-gated pipeline attains a Top-3 hit rate of 45.95% on the fixed-test subset. On the nurse-consensus safety cohort, contraindication detection reaches a sensitivity of 71.93%, a specificity of 71.15%, and a conditional unsafe-recommendation rate of 14.71%. The full safety-gated pipeline runs at 9–11 s per case on low-cost hardware costing about USD 700. These results demonstrate that safety-oriented, interpretable, and clinically deployable venipuncture decision support is technically feasible without specialized equipment. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
