	abstract = {Facing the consumption and operational challenges from high-penetration wind and PV integration, this study develops a storage planning method that jointly considers intelligent prediction and cooperative optimization for medium-voltage distribution networks. Traditional planning research mostly focuses on the capacity configuration of energy storage and fails to systematically address the core issue of the collaborative optimization of "site selection - capacity determination". In view of the above limitations, this study constructed a two-stage planning framework. Firstly, To obtain accurate short-term forecasts for wind and solar power, the Extreme Learning Machine (ELM) artificial intelligence algorithm is introduced, providing reliable input data for subsequent optimization and effectively enhancing the accuracy of the planning premise. Secondly, an optimization model for the location and capacity determination of energy storage stations in 35kV and below medium-voltage distribution networks, aiming at maximizing the comprehensive benefits of the system, was established. This model balances economic considerations (encompassing storage lifecycle costs and grid upgrade deferral value) with strict safety constraints, including system power balance, equipment operational boundaries, and energy storage SOC limits. Through simulation verification of the improved IEEE 30 nodes, the schemes planned by the method proposed in this paper can significantly enhance the peak shaving adequacy of the system, effectively alleviate branch congestion, and promote the local consumption of new energy. © 2026 IEEE.},
