	doi = {10.1016/j.displa.2026.103720},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053849705?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Face image quality assessment (FIQA) is an important preprocessing stage in face recognition systems. However, most existing methods output only a deterministic quality score and therefore do not characterize the reliability of individual predictions; fully fine-tuning a Vision Transformer backbone is also computationally expensive. To address these limitations, we combine parameter-efficient fine-tuning with heteroscedastic Gaussian modeling. The method unfreezes only the top two Transformer blocks, the final normalization layer, and a dual-output quality head of ViT-FIQA, and predicts the quality mean and variance using a bounded heteroscedastic Gaussian negative log-likelihood. The trainable parameters account for 8.29% of the full checkpoint. On CGFIQA-40 K with three random seeds, the model achieved an SRCC of 0.8030, compared with 0.8043 for the MSE baseline. Relative to the homoscedastic model, the Gaussian negative log-likelihood decreased from −1.585 to −1.714; empirical coverage of the 90% prediction interval was 89.65%, while mean interval width decreased by 26.3%. The pAURC<sub>0.2</sub> based on predictive variance was 0.0590, outperforming the 0.0736 obtained with random rejection. These results indicate that the proposed method provides sample-level uncertainty useful for risk ranking and calibration while retaining comparable point-prediction performance with a reduced trainable-parameter count and recorded peak allocated GPU-memory requirement under the reported setup. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
