	abstract = {Large language models frequently generate hallucinations—confident but factually unsupported claims—undermining their deployment in high-stakes applications. While existing approaches treat hallucination as an afterthought addressed through post-hoc filtering, we present E<sup>3</sup>-Guarded Generation, a unified architecture that embeds verification directly into the generation process through three complementary principles: Evidence grounding, logical Entailment, and Executable validation. Our architecture integrates five specialized components—Provenance-First Decoding, Speculative Verification Decoding, Counterfactual Cross-Examination, KWIK-Calibrated Abstention, and Semantic Constraint Grammars—within a carefully orchestrated pipeline that enforces faithfulness constraints at every generation stage. We present the complete system architecture, detail component interactions and information flow patterns, establish key design invariants, and analyze the architectural trade-offs between accuracy, latency, and coverage. The framework’s layered design enables modular deployment and graceful degradation through explicit feedback mechanisms. By shifting from reactive filtering to constructive correctness, E<sup>3</sup> demonstrates that hallucination prevention can be systematically engineered through principled architectural design, providing a foundation for trustworthy AI systems in critical domains. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
