	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105054118231?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Monocular depth predictors can fail locally under corruptions, nighttime scenes, and cross-dataset shifts, while downstream perception modules often consume the emitted dense depth map without a pixel-level reliability signal. This paper studies the post-hoc reliability layer available after a frozen monocular depth predictor has emitted its final map. The layer receives the emitted depth/disparity and low-cost RGB-derived boundary evidence, and estimates a local relative-depth risk ordering for selective depth use. We introduce ObsRank-GC, a compact geometry-conditioned ranker for this frozen-output cue interface. It constructs a deployment-observable cue tensor from normalized predicted geometry, image-gradient evidence, and geometry-gradient structure, and aggregates these cues with cue-state calibration and a local geometry-conditioned implicit risk query. A lightweight cue-perturbation term regularizes the training objective; the deployed module remains a single-forward post-hoc ranker. Over the full-domain protocol (KITTI-C S1–S5 averages, DrivingStereo, and NuScenes-Night across five frozen target predictors), ObsRank-GC obtains an average AURC of 0.00960 and a Risk@60 of 0.01997 with 0.023 M parameters and 0.224 GMACs at 192×640 resolution. It improves over static geometry scores, a shallow same-output learned scorer, and multi-forward test-time consistency under the frozen-predictor setting. Same-interface capacity diagnostics place ObsRank-GC at a compact cost–risk operating point relative to larger dense controls under the low-order cue budget. The result is an interface-aware evaluation protocol and a compact post-hoc reliability layer for already produced monocular depth outputs. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
