	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055684047?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Diabetes remains a prevalent chronic disease, where unreliable risk estimation may lead to missed diagnoses and unsafe clinical decisions. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an uncertainty-aware diabetes risk prediction framework integrating pseudo-missing value processing, MICE imputation, logistic regression, probability calibration, and a selective rejection mechanism based on confidence and predictive entropy. Experiments on the Pima Indian Diabetes Dataset show that MICE with logistic regression achieves an AUC of 0.8330. Results further demonstrate that selective rejection improves prediction reliability, with higher rejection rates consistently enhancing retained high-confidence predictions. Among calibration methods, Temperature Scaling and the raw model provide stable overall performance, while Isotonic Regression achieves the best reliability in selective screening scenarios (F1 = 0.7385, ECE = 0.0688 at 30% rejection). The proposed framework improves predictive performance, calibration reliability, and uncertainty awareness, offering a practical strategy for balancing diagnostic coverage and decision confidence. Our code is available at https://github.com/YienC/Diabetes-prediction. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
