	doi = {10.18517/IJASEIT.16.4.14360},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053989807?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence systems are being increasingly embedded into domains where design has been a key pillar, with little guidance from designers on how to operationalize trust within design practices. This work introduces the Trustworthy HumanCentered AI (THC-AI) design conceptual framework, which provides an organized set of considerations for designers to embed trustworthiness throughout the design lifecycle. We propose a five-step THC-AI design process (Empathy, Define, Ideate, Design, and Impact) based on an integrative review of human–computer interaction literature on trust and the existing AI governance and guidance frameworks, including the EU AI Act, ISO/IEC 24028, NIST AI Risk Management Framework, OECD AI Principles, and IEEE Ethically Aligned Design. The framework classifies fifteen trustworthiness attributes by when to prioritize them during the design process and links them to relevant design tools and stakeholders. It emphasizes a design-operational perspective on building trustworthy AI by recognizing the temporal and path dependency of trustworthiness attributes. We illustrate the framework's application in a healthcare clinical decision support use case. We also discuss how deferring fairness considerations to later design stages may limit downstream system performance in a fairness-deferral scenario. The framework's additional contributions include an organized classification of fairness notions and guidance in choosing individual-, group-, and counterfactual fairness categories for designers. Overall, it provides a practical foundation for designers and informs future work on empirical evaluations of the framework in generative AI and cross-cultural design scenarios. © 2026, IJASEIT is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0 International License.},
	type = {Article},
