	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048968325?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Rapid post-earthquake screening of damaged buildings is critical for search-and-rescue prioritization, safety zoning, and early reconstruction planning, yet expert visual inspection remains slow and difficult to scale. We present a deploymentoriented multimodal framework that combines ground-level RGB imagery with vibration and acoustic measurements to estimate ordinal building risk on edge devices. The model integrates lightweight modality-specific encoders, compact late fusion, an all-threshold ordinal prediction head, and uncertainty-aware selective prediction via Monte Carlo dropout and temperature scaling. Because paired field recordings are unavailable, the sensor channels are generated through physics-based simulations conditioned on damage severity; the study should therefore be interpreted as a controlled proof of concept rather than a fully field-validated multimodal benchmark. On the hybrid evaluation set, the proposed model achieves 87.3% accuracy with a mean ordinal error of 0.18, while uncertainty-based abstention improves selective accuracy to 91.4% at 90% coverage. Edge profiling shows real-time single-pass inference with latencies of 50 ms on Jetson Xavier AGX and 120 ms on Jetson Nano. These results support the feasibility of uncertainty-aware multimodal ordinal screening under embedded deployment constraints. © 2026 IEEE.},
