	abstract = {The evolution of mission-critical services and governmental satellite communication systems toward multi-layer space-air-ground networks introduces significant challenges in traffic management and service reliability. Traditional reactive network control mechanisms are inadequate for handling highly dynamic and bursty traffic patterns across heterogeneous segments, including LEO satellite systems, UAV-based non-terrestrial networks, and terrestrial $\text{5 G}$ infrastructures. This work presents an AI-native architecture that integrates Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-based traffic prediction within a Software-Defined Networking (SDN) control loop, enabling proactive network orchestration. A system model is developed to capture prediction-aware traffic steering and resource allocation. The proposed approach prioritizes mission-critical traffic, while leveraging caching and deferral mechanisms for delay-tolerant flows under congestion conditions. Furthermore, a practical ordered round-robin load balancing and scheduling algorithm is introduced, avoiding reliance on real-time optimization. A system-level simulator is also developed, incorporating beyond-5G/6G radio environments across ground, air, and space segments using 3GPP-based channel models. Simulation results demonstrate improved network stability, reduced packet loss, and enhanced resource utilization, highlighting the potential of predictive control for next-generation mission-critical service provisioning. © 2026 IEEE.},
