	abstract = {Post hoc uncertainty scoring supports selective prediction in lightweight image classification, but predictive entropy and gradient-based sensitivity have typically been evaluated in isolation, leaving their relative strengths across dataset regimes and under distribution shift insufficiently characterized. This study empirically compares predictive entropy, input gradient norm (GradNorm), and a simple normalized hybrid combination of the two across three lightweight CNN architectures (ShuffleNetV2 (0.5x), MobileNetV3-Small, and EfficientNetV2-S) on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and Tiny ImageNet and their corrupted benchmarks. Uncertainty scores are computed post hoc without retraining, ensembling, or architectural modification, and performance is assessed using AUROC, Expected Calibration Error (ECE), and retained accuracy at fixed coverage levels, with AvUC used only as corroborating evidence. Results show a regime-dependent ranking: GradNorm is strongest on CIFAR-10, whereas predictive entropy dominates on CIFAR-100 and Tiny ImageNet, and this pattern largely persists under corruption-based distribution shift. At fixed coverage, method choice changes retained accuracy by up to 18.75 percentage points. The fixed-weight hybrid remains competitive but does not consistently outperform the best single method. These findings indicate that, for the lightweight CNNs and corruption benchmarks evaluated here, the relative utility of entropy and GradNorm depends on dataset regime. © 2026 IEEE.},
