	abstract = {Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) studies often evaluate individual modules, leaving unclear how evidence-requirement complexity is associated with pipeline bottlenecks and end-to-end performance. Using 138 geothermal engineering documents, we constructed a knowledge base and an L1-L4 test set of 380 questions. Three strategies at each of four stages—chunking, retrieval, query processing, and generation—formed 81 pipelines and 30,780 answers. Evaluation combined question-level bootstrap inference, within-question paired tests, and blinded human validation. As required evidence expanded from local facts to cross-document synthesis, the main retrieval bottleneck shifted from finding some relevant evidence to covering the complete evidence set: Any-hit@5 decreased from 0.9193 at L1 to 0.7704 at L4, whereas All-hit@5 fell to 0.3057. Iterative querying benefited mainly multi-evidence tasks. Sparse and Hybrid significantly outperformed Dense, while Hybrid did not significantly outperform Sparse; Semantic chunking's intrinsic advantages did not yield stable retrieval gains. Citation improved Correctness, Direct retained higher Completeness, and Compression increased latency and token use without corresponding quality gains. Under a strict pure-abstention definition, degradation at higher evidence-requirement levels primarily reflected lower Completeness rather than more abstention. High-performing configurations were descriptively concentrated around Structure-based chunking, Hybrid retrieval, and Iterative querying at higher levels. These patterns motivate evidence-requirement-aware configuration as a design direction for specialized-literature RAG rather than establishing a validated adaptive router. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
