	abstract = {Standard neural classifiers used in Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) require all inputs to be mapped to a flat list of attack labels, potentially resulting in silent, highconfidence errors and precluding unknown attack detection. We propose HiNeS-IDS (Hierarchical Neural-Selective Intrusion Detection System), a novel dual-head network, which jointly trains its coarse and fine classifiers with hierarchical consistency, ensuring no taxonomy violations in its prediction. In detail, HiNeS-IDS coarse classifiers produce both family-level attack classifications and alerts, allowing abstention and escalation to analysts, rather than relying solely on blind high-confidence predictions. Three experimental results directly validate this design choice. (i) Graceful degradation: On the same data points where flat classifiers silently produce only $65-71 %$ fine-grained accuracy, HiNeS-IDS obtains 98-99% accurate attack family predictions - a 27-33 percentage point improvement in terms of actionable partial predictions. (ii) Emergent open-set detection: Without any OOD training, HiNeS-IDS correctly alarms 62% of novel attack traffic and correctly predicts 69.5% of novel attack traffic into the correct attack family based on hierarchical embedding geometry - a 60 percentage point improvement over a flat softmax classifier. Our framework supports a wide variety of network protocols, due to its level of abstraction, leading to significant performance improvements in practice compared to flat classification.  © 2026 IEEE.},
