	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048090953?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Bulgarian National Health Insurance Fund (NHIF) maintains a large administrative database of ambulatory records, each stored as an XML document representing a single outpatient visit. Sick leave is embedded as a section within the ambulatory XML, and the record is structurally identical regardless of whether it belongs to a genuinely ill patient or to a healthy caregiver. In the minority of cases where the sick person has no ambulatory visit - following hospitalization, emergency treatment, or similar circumstances - the caregiver's own GP issues an ambulatory record on behalf of the caregiver, carrying the diagnosis of the sick relative rather than Z76.3 (Healthy person accompanying sick person), which physicians rarely use in practice. This structural ambiguity introduces noise that must be removed prior to analysis, particularly when constructing disease registries for rare conditions.This paper proposes a two-stage pipeline combining keyword-based candidate filtering with local large language model classification using Gemma 4, augmented by an age-based exclusion rule grounded in Bulgarian social security legislation. Applied to a manually annotated gold standard of 1,400 records drawn from the 2014 NHIF dataset, the pipeline achieved precision of 0.927, recall of 0.967, and F1 of 0.947 for caregiver identification, with a model abstention rate of 0.14%.The proposed methodology provides a validated approach to a previously unaddressed data quality problem, directly applicable to longitudinal analysis of Bulgarian administrative health data.  © 2026 IEEE.},
