	abstract = {The rough set-based conflict analysis method provides efficient decision support for management-type problems with uncertainty and inconsistency. This paper innovatively incorporates the abstention factor into the methodology, constructs a four-valued conflict situation, defines four types of binary relationships, characterizes their properties and semantics, establishes a new conflict metric, and ultimately forms a comprehensive conflict analysis model based on the four-valued conflict situation. Subsequently, this paper applies the four-valued conflict situation to a three-way conflict analysis model. Through differentiated decision strategies, it achieves targeted handling of abstention values and constructs a weighted conflict distance metric formula, ultimately forming a three-way conflict analysis model based on the four-valued conflict situation. Compared to traditional three-valued conflict situations, the innovation of four-valued conflict situations lies in explicitly introducing the abstention factor. This factor is attributed to realistic considerations such as strategic adjustments and balancing of interests, providing actors with greater flexibility in expressing their positions and making the model more closely aligned with complex decision-making scenarios. Thus, this paper not only advances and enriches existing three-valued conflict models but also expands the theoretical boundaries and application domains of rough set-based conflict analysis methods, providing more suitable analytical tools for addressing conflicts in complex management scenarios. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.},
