	doi = {10.1109/WACVW68408.2026.00041},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047519551?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Childhood malnutrition affects over 150 million children (under 5) globally, yet automated screening systems suffer from overconfident predictions, inability to defer ambiguous cases, and misalignment with clinical workflows. We present a two-stage reinforcement learning framework formulating malnutrition screening as a contextual bandit with explicit deferral. Stage 1 classifies children as healthy, malnourished, or deferred; Stage 2 assigns subtypes when needed. The agent observes fused normalized anthropometric measurements and mean-pooled multi-view CLIP embeddings, with Monte Carlo dropout providing uncertainty estimates guiding deferral decisions. The reward function encodes clinical priorities through false-negative penalties and severity-weighted subtype scoring. Evaluated on 2,389 children with eight pose views, our method achieves 94.3% accuracy and 97.2% specificity, reducing false positive rate by 53.5% versus the best baseline while lowering expert deferral by a relative change of 25.4%. Precision on autonomously resolved cases improves by 5.8%, indicating reduced deferral reflects improved decision quality rather than overconfidence. Robustness analysis confirms stable performance under missing modalities and anthropometric noise, while fairness evaluation shows consistent behavior across gender and age groups. These results demonstrate that reinforcement learning with uncertainty-aware deferral substantially improves deployment efficiency for pediatric malnutrition screening in resource-constrained settings.  © 2026 IEEE.},
