	doi = {10.1109/ICWS72778.2026.00100},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051052551?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable knowledge graph (KG) reasoning is increasingly important for service ecosystems driven by application programming interfaces (APIs). In such ecosystems, typed relational graphs can represent service capabilities, dependencies, and invocation relationships, and link prediction can inform downstream service selection decisions. Existing KG reasoning models, particularly link prediction models, achieve strong ranking performance but do not produce calibrated trust scores, incorporate provenance directly into reasoning, or quantify structural fragility. Together, these limitations reduce their usefulness for deployment decisions. This paper proposes PRISM (Provenance, Robustness, and Inference-based Service trust Mechanism), a trust-aware framework for selective KG link prediction. The framework uses Neural Bellman-Ford Networks as a path-based inference backbone and derives three complementary trust signals: epistemic uncertainty, provenance quality, and counterfactual fragility. These signals are combined through a learned query-adaptive aggregator into a single calibrated trust score that supports selective prediction, allowing a reasoner to abstain on low-trust link predictions under a user-defined precision target. Evaluated on benchmark knowledge graphs, PRISM achieves strong calibration with an Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.034 and reaches Hits@1=0.943 on Hetionet (from a baseline of 0.102) in the high-confidence regime (τ=0.9), suitable for selective decisions where coverage is deliberately traded for precision. At broader coverage levels, PRISM improves Hits@1 by up to 31% on FB15k-237. These findings suggest that PRISM provides a practical basis for trust-aware selective reasoning in dependency-rich service ecosystems. Code is available on GitHub.<sup>1</sup><sup>1</sup>https://github.com/parimehrmorassa/PRISM.git  © 2026 IEEE.},
