	abstract = {—Hybrid quantum–classical detectors are usually evaluated as monolithic alternatives to classical models, which conflates two separable questions: whether the hard cases can be identified, and whether a quantum model can resolve them. We introduce Q-RAD, a gated residual architecture that separates these questions by construction. A quantum-blind, quantum-cost-free gating function routes only ambiguous transactions to a financial-transaction-specific quantum-kernel branch, whose scores are fused residually with a classical trunk under an explicit finite-shot budget. We predeclare the architecture, an eight-comparison confirmatory family with Holm–Bonferroni correction, a cost metric in quantum circuit executions, and a refutation posture, then evaluate on the Kaggle Credit-Card Fraud dataset over ten repeated case-control splits under three blocking validity gates, including exact-equality replay of pre-viously published results. The routing half of the architecture succeeds: the gate attains 0.889 AUROC against realized classical errors and captures 77% of them within a 22% routing budget. The quantum half fails: handed exactly those hard cases, the quantum residual produced zero net corrections in nine of ten seeds and one net regression in the tenth. Q-RAD attains mean case-control test F1 0.916 against 0.918 for the classical trunk and 0.922 for an otherwise identical classical second opinion; the hypotheses of improvement over the classical trunk, over random routing, and over a classical second opinion are all refuted after correction. We report this refutation as predeclared, and localise it: ambiguity in this task is identifiable from classical signals alone, but the quantum kernel cannot exploit that signal at any tested shot budget. Balanced-cohort metrics are benchmark values, not deployment-prevalence estimates. © The Author(s). This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY 4.0,