	abstract = {Multi-label chest X-ray screening systems are important in the clinical workflow, and confidence is a necessity when deploying this technology. A model that is highly discriminating may still be miscalibrated to produce overconfident errors which decrease safety and trust. We learn about confidence-sensitive multi-label screening on the NIH ChestX-ray14 data set through the combination of post-hoc calibration and selective prediction analysis. On an initially reproducible ConvNeXt multi-label baseline, we initially report good discrimination performance, with best validation macro AUC of 0.835 and micro AUC of 0.883, and test micro of 0.848 and test macro of 0.796. We also present bootstrap uncertainty of the summary measures, where AUC means equal 0.796 (95 percent interval: 0.7890.803). Thereafter we perform temperature scaling to calibrate logits on a held-out validation set, and evaluate reliability with metrics of calibration and reliability diagrams. We use safe usage under uncertainty by analyzing selective prediction in risk- coverage curves to understand how not using in situations of uncertainty can limit expected risk at the cost of controlling the proportion of predictions retained. Generally, the paper presents a fully operationalized confidence assessment protocol of multi-label screening of chest X-rays with insights on both practical calibration and selective deployment.  © 2026 IEEE.},
