	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051298729?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Activation-space interventions steer language model behavior by editing hidden states along a concept direction. Existing methods usually ask how strongly to edit, but not whether the direction is reliable enough to edit at all. This is an information-reliability problem: an inferred internal signal is being used to modify generated information, and a mistaken edit can silently reduce utility, invert the target behavior, or introduce cross-concept side effects. We present a preflight diagnostic fingerprint for activation editing, (κ * , dc, A), measuring task-subspace coherence, direction quality, and projection asymmetry. The fingerprint produces a calibrated intervention scale that can be zero, making abstention a firstclass output rather than a failed edit. On GPT-2, the gated projection achieves +53.6% toxicity reduction using five-seed generation means (p < 0.001) while incurring 3.3 less perplexity cost than ungated projection. On gender, the same framework refuses to edit because the direction is one-sided; removing the asymmetry factor over-corrects pronoun skew from 0.704 to 0.017. On Mistral-7B, the framework abstains from projection at all 32 layers (κ * = 40), while an ActAdd sweep can still reduce toxicity, showing that the diagnosis is operator-specific rather than a claim that the concept is uneditable. The contribution is not a stronger universal edit, but a reliability layer that explains when projection is safe, when it should be weakened, and when it should not be used.  © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 0; All Open Access; Hybrid Gold Open Access}
