	abstract = {Recognizing emotions from speech is still difficult due to the influence of emotions on the various parts of the conversation (acoustic, lexical, and emotional) and the differences in emotion across different speakers. This paper introduces a multimodal framework, namely ASPIRE-SER, that is adaptive, combining the WavLM acoustic representations with CNN-BiGRU spectro-temporal features, prosodic descriptors, RoBERTa transcript semantics, and speaker-aware graph reasoning. The use of a sample-conditioned gating mechanism dynamically weights complementary evidence and temperature scaling, and confidence-aware abstention enhance decision reliability. ASPIRE-SER performed at 76.82%, 77.96%, 76.87% and 0.708 accuracy (unweighted, weighted, macro-F1, and MCC), respectively, based on leave-one-session-out validation on the four-class IEMOCAP setting. The expected calibration error was reduced from 4.31% to 1.87% with calibration and 81.39% accuracy at 95% coverage (selective prediction). © 2026 IEEE.},
