	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ASSIC70037.2026.11717218},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055493767?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Object detection technology, used for extracting information like different objects, spatial coordinates and category of objects from images or videos, provides an important insight in the field of computer vision. However it is observed that these object detection model fails drastically under adverse conditions, which could be fatal when used in real life applications like Autonomous vehicles. We show this collapse using how several metrics like Accuracy, Entropy, Brier score changes for clean vs degraded environmental conditions. Finally a customized Risk score is calculated involving visibility, detections and Combined Reliability Score to make the system failure aware and perform selective predictions.In this research, we used two object detection model to test if solution can be Model agnostic, for that we used YOLOv5 and YOLOv8 architectures. We used subset from BDD100K dataset, which includes 100 images for each of the four conditions: clear, fog, rain, and night to observe the model behavior. Accuracy improved from approx. 80% to 83% for YOLOv5 and from 87% to 90% for YOLOv8 when selective predictions (Safe + Moderate Risk) are considered. The proposed solution helps to detect risky scenarios which makes the model failure-aware and improves security when used in Autonomous system. © 2026 IEEE.},
