	doi = {10.1109/DCOSS-IoT69657.2026.00088},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049629298?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {While deep neural networks are highly susceptible to adversarial attacks, many defence mechanisms require retraining, architectural changes, or sophisticated optimisation processes. In this work, we present a post-processing defence system that can be used at inference time as an external decision layer for a fixed pretrained classifier. The proposed defence mechanism follows a simple three-stage pipeline; it uses a median filter-based recovery stage to partly mitigate adversarial attacks, a statistical residual detector using class-conditional Mahalanobis distances for input evaluation, and finally a confidence-aware selective prediction stage to accept or reject a prediction. The defense mechanism improves the robustness of a given classifier against gradient-based adversarial attacks without retraining or modifying it. The experimental results show that the proposed defense mechanism improves post-attack classification accuracy with risk-coverage trade-offs while maintaining high accuracy for accepted samples under extreme distribution shift. Due to its simplicity and interpretability, the proposed defense mechanism can be used in resource-constrained environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
