	abstract = {The Model Context Protocol (MCP) enables LLMmediated querying and synthesis across distributed FHIR servers but lacks builtin guarantees for provenance, tamperproof auditing, and granular patient consent. We present a trust layer that integrates permissioned blockchain smart contracts, verifiable credentials (VCs), and decentralized identifiers (DIDs) with MCPenabled FHIR workflows. Our architecture immutably logs MCP queries and hashes of returned FHIR bundles, enforces patientcentric consent via onchain policies, and returnsss verifiable audit trails alongside narrative summaries. We detail contract interfaces, threat model, and deployment options (L2/sidechains; ZK attestations). Using Synthea derived cohorts, we benchmark endtoend latency, throughput, consentrevocation latency, and audit verifiability against three baselines (OAuth2/UMA, PKI audit logs, and TEE attestations). Results show comparable latency (p95<250 ms added), deterministic audit recovery (100 %), and materially faster consent revocation propagation (p501.8 s) with modest cost (< 1 per 1k queries on L2). We discuss risks from LLM indeterminism and introduce grounding, constrained decoding, and abstention policies. The approach advances interoperable, patientcontrolled AI summarization with verifiable trust and practical deployment pathways.  © 2025 IEEE.},
