	abstract = {In industrial production environments, printer operations within production networks are subject to stringent constraints imposed by production processes. Due to the distinctive characteristics of printer protocols, traditional port scanning and fingerprinting methods are prone to triggering spurious printing or service disruptions, potentially leading to production incidents. This paper proposes a three-tier adaptive identification framework integrating lightweight fingerprinting, behavioral profiling, and machine learning, coupled with read-only security plugins and auditable decision pathways. In production environments encompassing more than 3,000 devices (including 500+ printers) and control settings, the framework achieves a misidentification rate below 0.3% while maintaining zero spurious printing, and constrains the update latency for vulnerable assets within 300 seconds. Furthermore, we introduce a selective identification mechanism with conservative abstention based on conformal prediction, whichsafely handles boundary samples at confidence level 1-α, demonstrating the feasibility of mitigating risks associated with deep protocol interactions.  © 2025 IEEE.},
