	abstract = {The economic evaluation of Intelligent Transportation Management Systems (ITMS) remains challenging due to the complexity of quantifying socio-economic benefits across diverse urban contexts. This study develops a comprehensive evaluation framework that combines benefit-cost analysis with traffic safety assessment methodologies to address current gaps in systematic ITMS evaluation. The research employs a quasi-experimental design based on the odds ratio method to isolate ITMS-induced effects from external confounding factors, and applies a benefit–loss ratio approach to quantify economic outcomes across four dimensions: driving cost reduction, travel time savings, infrastructure investment deferral, and indirect industrial spillovers. Results indicate that ITMS implementation generated 3.47 billion yuan in annual driving cost savings, 32 yuan per hour in per capita travel time value, and 40.14 billion yuan in deferred infrastructure expenditures. The methodology offers transportation authorities and urban planners a systematic approach for ITMS investment decision-making, though further validation across diverse urban contexts and integration with existing transportation planning frameworks remains necessary. © 2025, Success Culture Press. All rights reserved.},
