	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/IMNS67862.2026.11655307},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049144386?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Electrocardiogram (ECG) machine learning models often report strong benchmark performance, yet reliability can degrade across hospitals, devices, lead configurations, and patient populations. This paper surveys 55 ECG machine learning studies from 2020 to 2026 through the lens of dataset shift, evaluation practice, and reliability assessment. We organize the literature around covariate, label, and concept shift and audit deploymentrelevant evaluation designs, including cross-dataset testing, external validation, institution-level holdout, and lead or device variation. Cross-dataset evaluation appears in 67.3% of reviewed studies and external validation in 45.5%, but institution-level holdout appears in only 16.4%. Reliability-oriented evaluation remains limited: calibration and uncertainty estimation each appear in 5.5% of studies, selective prediction in 1.8%, robustness testing in 14.5%, and fairness analysis in 3.6%. The central finding is direct: Architectural progress has advanced faster than deployment-oriented evaluation, and benchmark accuracy alone is not sufficient evidence of clinical readiness. © 2026 IEEE.},
