	abstract = {Financial fraud detection remains challenging because transaction data are highly imbalanced, fraud patterns evolve rapidly, and reliable decision-making is required in high-stakes financial environments. This paper proposes ARGUS, an uncertainty-aware fraud-detection framework that integrates a hybrid Transformer-based model with gated token mixing with Bayesian Monte Carlo dropout and conformal risk control. The proposed architecture combines tokenised tabular embedding, multi-head self-attention, dilated convolution, and gated depthwise-convolutional token mixing to capture both global and local feature dependencies. Predictive uncertainty is estimated using Monte Carlo dropout, whereas the conformal-style selective prediction allows the model to drop out on transactions with low confidence and subsequently minimize the risk of decision-making. The results of the experiments conducted on the Credit Card Fraud Detection dataset indicate that ARGUS has an area under the precision-recall curve of 0.880, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.975, F 1 -score of 0.860, a precision of 0.910 and a recall of 0.820. Besides this, the proposed framework has a coverage of 0.88 and produces selective risk of 0.015, which is much better than both the classical machine learning and recent deep learning baselines. The analysis of ablation also demonstrates that the combination of Bayesian uncertainty and conformal risk control leads to the decrease of the risk to 0.181, 0.082, and 0.015. These findings indicate that ARGUS not only offers highly accurate fraud-detection capabilities but also efficient, understandable and risk-sensitive decision support to support viable financial usages.  © 2013 IEEE.},
