	abstract = {The rise of mobile phishing via SMS (smishing) has become a pressing cybersecurity concern, particularly in multilingual regions like South Asia where messages frequently appear in Bangla, English, or their noisy code-mixed forms. Traditional models often deliver high accuracy in controlled settings but fail when confronted with real-world challenges such as spelling variations, transliteration, distributional shifts, or out-of-distribution (OOD) attacks. In this work, we present a dualview XLM-R framework that jointly learns from both original and romanized Bangla SMS, enforcing prediction consistency across views to build resilience against orthographic variation. To enhance trustworthiness, we incorporate probability calibration through temperature scaling, evaluate selective classification with risk-coverage analysis, and benchmark multiple OOD detection strategies including Energy, MSP, MC-Dropout, and Mahalanobis distance. Experiments on a newly curated Bangla-English code-mixed dataset show that our model achieves over 99.06% accuracy, near-perfect calibration (ECE ≈ 0.005), and reliable selective abstention. While Energy and MSP struggle under OOD conditions, Mahalanobis distance reaches AUROC ≈ 0.996, offering strong open-set robustness. These findings highlight not only superior performance over strong baselines but also improved reliability and transparency, positioning our approach as a practical and trustworthy solution for smishing defense in low-resource, multilingual contexts. © 2025 IEEE.},
