	abstract = {Ensemble classifying methods and confidence-fusion of models are a growing trend for fraud detection systems. The use of an ensemble approach permits the use of models that would have otherwise gone unutilized. Although current research shows that these strategies can be successful, the conditions under which frameworks have been ineffective or have not produced results have yet to be sufficiently studied. This research paper provides an empirical study of a Confidence-Based Selective Activation Framework for credit card fraud detection. This framework consists of combining a Calibrated XGBoost algorithm with a Focal Loss Neural Network used in conjunction with Meta-Feature Based Uncertainty Quantification. Using the strict Anti-Leakage Procedures applied to the 2013 Credit Card Fraud Dataset, we provide an analysis of when confidence extensions improve performance and when they do not. Although the confidence-based selective activation framework evaluates improved performance based on (Δ AUC-PR ≤ 0.0002), increased computational demand, in instances where the primary classifier has been well calibrated, and the data distribution remains constant, the selective activation framework was unable to provide any significant improvements. We show that through the use of Principal Component Analysis (PCA), uncertainty products resulting from the utilisation of Meta-Features become redundant in compressed feature spaces; dynamic thresholds provide no advantage over static thresholds; and Fusion Weights remain insensitive to large ranges. Empirical data from these studies show the limitations of Confidence-Based Methods and provide Evidence-Based Recommendations for practitioners on when to use simpler, single model solutions. © 2026 IEEE.},
