	abstract = {Although deep learning models for computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) of breast cancer in mammography have demonstrated strong discriminative performance, their clinical adoption is still restricted because of overconfident predictions and a lack of mechanisms to convey predictive reliability. One promising approach to addressing this lack of trust is uncertainty quantification, or UQ. However, the landscape of UQ techniques specifically used for breast cancer CAD is dispersed among various clinical scenarios, methodological families, and evaluation protocols. A targeted critical review of uncertainty quantification techniques for breast cancer CAD in mammography is presented in this paper. We suggest an organized taxonomy that divides current techniques into four families: evidential deep learning, Bayesian approximation, calibration-based post-hoc methods, and selective prediction frameworks. We assess clinical readiness, look at representative mammography applications, and analyze the fundamental ideas for each family. We provide a comparative analysis in a number of significant domains, including computational cost, uncertainty type coverage, and deployment suitability. Based on the identified gaps, we offer a particular research agenda that tackles the need for multi-view uncertainty fusion, uncertainty-aware explainability, standardized UQ benchmarks, and clinical validation studies. This review aims to guide researchers and practitioners toward the development of more dependable and clinically useful CAD systems for breast cancer. © 2026 IEEE.},
