	title = {Uncertainty-Aware Deep Ensemble Learning with Integrated Gradients for Reliable Dragon Fruit Maturity Classification},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {GSEACT 2026 - 2026 IEEE Global Symposium on Emerging and Communication Technologies},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/GSEACT68539.2026.11620508},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047947381?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Automated harvesting, grading, and post-harvest practices in precision agriculture depend on accurate assessments of fruit ripeness. Vision-based classification systems used to determine fruit maturity can be overly confident in their results if trained with limited data sets and applied in the unpredictable conditions found in the field. This study presents an uncertaintyaware deep learning system for classifying the ripeness of dragon fruits using both Deep Ensemble Learning (DEL) and Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI). The backbone of the model was developed using the pre-trained EfficientNet architecture, which was modified to include attention mechanisms to better focus on the region of interest (ROI), such as the fruit itself, and away from irrelevant background areas. Training occurred using a multi-task approach where two objectives were simultaneously optimized; namely, predicting whether or not the fruit has reached a ripe state, and estimating how mature the fruit is by calculating a continuous maturity index. A measure of epistemic uncertainty was calculated through disagreement between the groups, specifically through the use of mutual information, allowing the system to selectively reject samples with estimates of low confidence. In experiments conducted on a balanced set of images representing a wide range of stages of maturity of dragon fruits, the model correctly classified all acceptable samples as ripe or unripe, while rejecting those whose classifications were accompanied by high levels of uncertainty. Visual explanations of the model's predictions were created by integrating gradients to support the conclusion that the model focuses on biologically relevant regions of the fruit as opposed to background artifacts. Ultimately, the proposed framework represents a significant advancement in the development of precision agricultural systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
